      "category": "correctness",
      "title": "Cancellation detection breaks when zenity emits any stderr warning",
      "message": "A zenity cancel (exit code 1) is only treated as cancellation when stderr is completely empty. GTK commonly writes harmless warnings such as Gtk-Message or theme/dbus notices to stderr. A user who cancels in that case gets a hard ProcessError instead of app.ErrCanceled. This contradicts the design in `truncatingBuffer`, which says warnings must not turn a chooser result into a failure.",
      "suggestion": "Treat exit code 1 as cancellation unless the diagnostic matches a known failure pattern, such as \"cannot open display\" or \"Unable to init server\". Alternatively, ignore GTK warning-prefixed lines when checking whether the diagnostic is empty. Add a test where the helper exits 1 with a non-fatal warning on stderr.",
